  "question": "What has been proposed to reduce the risk of training an AI to favor actions that humans would reject if they understood their consequences, and by what mechanism?",
  "reference_answer": "The proposed intervention is to solve Eliciting Latent Knowledge (ELK): get a model to honestly disclose what it knows through a Reporter that translates its internal world model into human concepts, rather than merely generating claims humans find convincing. This would inform human overseers about the consequences of the model’s actions, allowing them to give correct feedback. That feedback would create a reward signal humans would actually want the AI to maximize. ELK remains an open problem, rather than an established solution.",
  "spans": [
    "A part of the model acts as a Reporter that can answer queries about what the model knows.",
    "We want the Reporter to directly translate from the AI’s model of the world to human concepts, rather than just simulating what would be convincing to the human.",
    "This is an open problem that ARC considers as the core of the outer alignment problem.",
    "A solution to ELK would make the human overseer fully informed about the consequences of the model’s actions, enabling them to provide correct feedback, which creates a reward signal that we would actually be happy for an AI system to maximize."
